Reason:
The bug occurs when page table buffer objects (BOs) are evicted but not validated before updating the page tables. The first statement attempts to lock the page directory (PD) of the virtual memory (VM), and if the VM is still in an evicting state, it will return an error. The subsequent validation attempts on the BOs fail, leading to the `amdgpu_vm_update_range` function returning -EBUSY, which causes the `restore_process_worker` to enter a dead loop.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
/* Validate BOs managed by KFD */
list_for_each_entry(mem, &process_info->kfd_bo_list, validate_list) {
    ret = amdgpu_amdkfd_bo_validate(bo, domain, false);
}
ret = process_validate_vms(process_info, &exec.ticket);
```
Reason:
The fix ensures that the BOs are validated before any updates to the page tables are attempted. The first statement validates the BOs managed by KFD, ensuring they are in a valid state before proceeding. The second statement validates the virtual memory (VM) itself, ensuring that it is ready for updates. This sequence prevents the VM from being in an evicting state when the page tables are updated, thus avoiding the -EBUSY error and the resulting dead loop.

